tl;dr: Reproduced stale data restoration from unprivileged userspace
without the patch. A kernel with this patch alone delivered the missing
userfaultfd notification and restored the expected zero page.

The test ran as UID 65534 with UFFD_USER_MODE_ONLY and
vm.unprivileged_userfaultfd=0. On A, MADV_GUARD_INSTALL returned 0 but
sent no REMOVE event. After guard removal, the userfaultfd handler reused
its saved copy and the page read back as 0x5a. On B, the call returned 0,
sent one REMOVE event, and the page read back as zero.

A guard spanning two adjacent registered VMAs likewise retained both
old bytes on A; B sent a bounded REMOVE event for each VMA and both
pages read as zero. Repeated guard installation sent no REMOVE events
on A and two on B. Guard faults still occurred.

I also delayed delivery of a REMOVE event while another thread unmapped
the page. On B, munmap completed first and MADV_GUARD_INSTALL returned
-1/ENOMEM after rechecking the VMA. File-backed asynchronous userfaultfd
write protection and an unregistered anonymous mapping passed their
guard/remove controls on both kernels.

Tested on x86-64 KVM. No kernel warning appeared in the retained output.

 mm/madvise.c |   13 +++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 13 insertions(+)

--- a/mm/madvise.c~mm-madvise-notify-userfaultfd-when-installing-guard-regions
+++ a/mm/madvise.c
@@ -1163,6 +1163,19 @@ static long madvise_guard_install(struct
 	if (!is_valid_guard_vma(vma, /* allow_locked = */false))
 		return -EINVAL;
 
+	/* userfaultfd_remove() can drop mmap_lock. */
+	if (!userfaultfd_remove(vma, range->start, range->end)) {
+		mark_mmap_lock_dropped(madv_behavior);
+		mmap_read_lock(madv_behavior->mm);
+		madv_behavior->vma = vma = vma_lookup(madv_behavior->mm,
+						    range->start);
+		if (!vma)
+			return -ENOMEM;
+		if (!is_valid_guard_vma(vma, /* allow_locked = */false))
+			return -EINVAL;
+		range->end = min(range->end, vma->vm_end);
+	}
+
